<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonian Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/214-bce">214</a>–<a href="/years/148-bce">148 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Macedonian Wars were a series of conflicts from 214 to 148 BCE in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and its Greek allies fought the major Greek kingdoms of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a> against Philip V, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, ended indecisively in 205 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated Philip at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> at Magnesia in the war of 192–188 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a> at Pydna in 168 BCE, after which Macedonia was split into four client republics. A fourth war against the pretender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a> was followed in 146 BCE by the defeat of the Achaean League and the destruction of Corinth, leaving Rome dominant over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, who sought to explain how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> came to dominate the Greek east in less than a century, traced the wars to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> that asked for Roman protection against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> after the weakening of Ptolemaic Egypt. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> was succeeded in 205 BCE by the five-year-old Ptolemy V, revolts broke out in Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> seceded, and Macedon and the Seleucids made an alliance to divide the Ptolemaic empire. Ambassadors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> evidence of the pact between Philip V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-macedonian-war">First Macedonian War</a>, Marcus Valerius Laevinus crossed the Adriatic with forces that, with allies from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, did little more than skirmish and seize minor coastal territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> wished only to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> busy while it fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-phoenice">Treaty of Phoenice</a> ended it in 205 BCE. In the Second Macedonian War Titus Quinctius Flamininus reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> by 198 BCE and defeated Philip at Cynoscephalae in 197 BCE. By the Treaty of Tempea Philip had to give up his recent Greek conquests, and at the Olympiad of 196 BCE Rome proclaimed the freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and withdrew its forces.</p><p>The Seleucid War followed, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> was a chief military adviser to the Seleucid emperor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> mobilised a Roman-Greek force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>. After Philip's death in 179 BCE his son Perseus moved against his neighbours, and the Senate declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> when he was implicated in a plot to assassinate an ally of Rome. Roman legions broke the Macedonian phalanx at Pydna in 168 BCE, and Macedonia was divided into four client republics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Fourth Macedonian War, from 150 to 148 BCE, was fought against a pretender, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriscus">Andriscus</a>, who tried to restore the kingdom, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> swiftly defeated the Macedonians at the second battle of Pydna. The Achaean League then mobilised against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 146 BCE and was quickly beaten. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> blames demagogues in the league's cities for inspiring a suicidal war. As an object lesson, Rome destroyed Corinth in the same year that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> was destroyed.</p><p>The most significant war was the one with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, and this war and those with Macedonia effectively ended both empires as major powers, though neither led at once to overt Roman domination. After the wars the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> stayed a shifting network of polities with differing degrees of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, with outright military rule in parts of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macedonian Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
